1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,500
Suppose a balance grows continuously at a
rate equal to its current amount per year.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,440
How long until it doubles?

3
00:00:12,067 --> 00:00:16,335
After a time t, its multiplier is
e to the t,

4
00:00:16,335 --> 00:00:20,507
so doubling asks when e to the t equals
two.

5
00:00:20,507 --> 00:00:26,627
The answer is the natural logarithm of
two, about zero point six nine years.

6
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:30,587
A logarithm finds the missing input of an
exponential.

7
00:00:30,587 --> 00:00:33,552
But what if the input also records
turns, and

8
00:00:33,552 --> 00:00:36,787
several different numbers lead to the same
point?

9
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:40,187
Then undoing the exponential needs a
choice.

10
00:00:40,187 --> 00:00:45,907
And when numbers become matrices, even the
question ‘can it be undone?’ gets subtler.

